How to Build a Minimal Skincare Routine on a Student Budget
College budgets don't leave much room for a 10-step skincare routine — and honestly, they don't need to. A good routine can run on four products, cost less than a single dinner out, and still do the job properly.
Start with a cleanser. This is the one step you shouldn't skip, morning or night, but you also don't need to overspend here. A basic gel or foaming cleanser under ₹250 works just as well as anything pricier, as long as it doesn't leave your skin feeling tight and stripped afterward. If it does, it's too harsh — swap it.
Next comes moisturizer, and this is where most people either skip a step or overcomplicate it. One moisturizer, used consistently, does more than five products used occasionally. Look for something lightweight if you have oily or combination skin, and slightly richer if your skin runs dry. The goal isn't the fanciest ingredient list — it's finishing the tube.
If you're going to add one active ingredient, make it sunscreen, not a serum. This is non-negotiable, and it's also the step students skip most. Sun damage is cumulative and mostly invisible until years later, so a broad-spectrum SPF 30-50, applied every single morning, is the highest-impact thing you can do for your skin — more than any serum will ever do.
Once cleanser, moisturizer, and sunscreen are locked in as a daily habit, consider one targeted product — a niacinamide serum for tone and texture, or a salicylic acid product for breakouts. Add it after the basics are consistent, not before. A five-step routine done inconsistently does less than a three-step routine done every day.
Skincare doesn't have to be expensive to work. It has to be consistent. Pick four products you can actually afford to repurchase every month, use them every day, and give it six weeks before deciding whether it's working. That's a routine that will outlast any trend — and your budget.
